Desktop on Linux: trust the system's certificate authorities in the main process too

"Test" on a saved connection (and every other main-process request) uses Node's built-in
certificate list on Linux, so a gateway behind a private or company certificate authority that
the system trusts is reported as "Connection test failed ... the live WebSocket (/api/ws)
connection failed", while the renderer (Chromium, which reads the system list) connects fine.

The comment in system-ca.ts says Linux is left out because the default trust already covers the
system store. It does not: Electron's Node starts from its bundled list. On Fedora 45,
tls.getCACertificates('system') returns the system store (363 certificates here, including a
private one installed with update-ca-trust), and merging it makes the test pass.

Tested on Hermes Desktop v0.23.0 (Electron 44.9.3, Node 24.22.0), Fedora 45: before, "Connection
test failed"; after, "Reachable", against a gateway whose certificate chains to a private
authority in the system store.

--- a/apps/desktop/electron/system-ca.ts
+++ b/apps/desktop/electron/system-ca.ts
@@ -14,9 +14,11 @@
 
 // Platforms whose OS trust store tls.getCACertificates('system') can enumerate: the Windows
 // cert store and the macOS keychain (Node reads user + System keychains there, honoring the
-// "Always Trust" SSL policy — Node ≥ 22.15). Linux is deliberately absent: its 'system' store
-// is the OpenSSL directory scan, which the default trust already covers.
-const SYSTEM_CA_PLATFORMS = new Set(['win32', 'darwin'])
+// "Always Trust" SSL policy — Node ≥ 22.15), and on Linux the distribution's store (the
+// OpenSSL directory and bundle). Electron's Node starts from its own bundled list, so without
+// this a certificate authority the admin added with update-ca-trust / update-ca-certificates
+// is trusted by the renderer but not by main-process requests such as the connection test.
+const SYSTEM_CA_PLATFORMS = new Set(['win32', 'darwin', 'linux'])
 
 function installSystemCaTrust(tlsApi: NodeTlsCaApi, platform = process.platform): SystemCaResult {
   if (!SYSTEM_CA_PLATFORMS.has(platform)) {
